{"version":"6.9","hitCount":3,"request":{"queryString":"(EXT_ID:26017442 AND SRC:MED) OR PMCID:PMC6716493 OR DOI:\"10.1016/s0140-6736(97)11096-0\" OR (EXT_ID:99999999 AND SRC:MED)","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":4,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"31507447","source":"MED","pmid":"31507447","pmcid":"PMC6716493","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6716493"]},"doi":"10.3389/fphys.2019.01083","title":"Vibration Cycling Did Not Affect Energy Demands Compared to Normal Cycling During Maximal Graded Test.","authorString":"Jemni M, Gu Y, Hu Q, Marina M, Fessi MS, Moalla W, Mkaouer B, Konukman F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jemni M","lastName":"Jemni","firstName":"Monèm","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5410-9085"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Sports Science, Ningbo University, Zhejiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gu Y","lastName":"Gu","firstName":"Yaodong","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Sports Science, Ningbo University, Zhejiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu Q","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Qiuli","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Sports Science, Ningbo University, Zhejiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Marina M","lastName":"Marina","firstName":"Michel","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7783-5178"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institut Nacional d'Educació Física de Catalunya, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Fessi MS","lastName":"Fessi","firstName":"Mohamed Saifeddin","initials":"MS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6785-9475"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UR EM2S, High Institute of Sport and Physical Education Sfax, University of Sfax, Sfax, Tunisia."}]}},{"fullName":"Moalla W","lastName":"Moalla","firstName":"Wassim","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UR EM2S, High Institute of Sport and Physical Education Sfax, University of Sfax, Sfax, Tunisia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mkaouer B","lastName":"Mkaouer","firstName":"Bessem","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1499-112X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Higher Institute of Sport and Physical Education of Ksar Saïd, Manouba University, Manouba, Tunisia."}]}},{"fullName":"Konukman F","lastName":"Konukman","firstName":"Ferman","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sport Science Program, College of Arts and Science, Qatar University, Doha, Qatar."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5410-9085"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6785-9475"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7783-5178"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1499-112X"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":2777617,"dateOfPublication":"2019 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Physiol","title":"Frontiers in physiology","essn":"1664-042X","issn":"1664-042X","nlmid":"101549006","isoabbreviation":"Front Physiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-01-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"1083","abstractText":"The aim of this study was to compare the physiological responses between a vibration induced cycling step protocol (Vib) and normal cycling (without vibration, no-Vib). Eighteen moderate trained males (age 24.1 ± 4.3 years; weight 76.5 ± 10.5 kg; height 178.0 ± 6.4 cm) have participated in this study. They randomly performed two gradual maximal exercise tests on two separate days using a new bike that automatically induces vibration cycling and the Corival cycle ergometer. The choice of two different bikes was made because of the impossibility to recreate the same power output without altering the cycling cadence on the vibration Bike. Both protocols were matched for power output and cycling cadence incrementations. Oxygen uptake (VO<sub>2</sub>), carbon dioxide production (VCO<sub>2</sub>), ventilation (VE), heart rate (HR), blood lactate and rating of perceived exertion (RPE) during each stage were continuously recorded. No statistical differences were founded for all variables when comparing the Vib to no-Vib trials, except a higher ventilation during the vibration trial at submaximal levels. The results of this study do not confirm those of previous studies stated that Vib increased metabolic demands during cycling exercise. Added vibration stimulus to an incremental cycling protocol does not affect physiological parameters.","affiliation":"Faculty of Sports Science, Ningbo University, Zhejiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ventilatory Threshold","Vo2max","Cycloergometer","Obla","Energy Demands"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fphys.2019.01083/pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2019.01083"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC6716493"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC6716493?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2019-09-12","dateOfRevision":"2020-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2019-08-23","firstPublicationDate":"2019-08-23","firstIndexDate":"2019-09-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"26017442","source":"MED","pmid":"26017442","doi":"10.1038/nature14539","title":"Deep learning.","authorString":"LeCun Y, Bengio Y, Hinton G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"LeCun Y","lastName":"LeCun","firstName":"Yann","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1] Facebook AI Research, 770 Broadway, New York, New York 10003 USA. [2] New York University, 715 Broadway, New York, New York 10003, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bengio Y","lastName":"Bengio","firstName":"Yoshua","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Operations Research Université de Montréal, Pavillon André-Aisenstadt, PO Box 6128 Centre-Ville STN Montréal, Quebec H3C 3J7, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Hinton G","lastName":"Hinton","firstName":"Geoffrey","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1] Google, 1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, California 94043, USA. [2] Department of Computer Science, University of Toronto, 6 King's College Road, Toronto, Ontario M5S 3G4, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9846-0176"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0308-2625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2540-2611"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["recommended_reviews","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7553","volume":"521","journalIssueId":2288264,"dateOfPublication":"2015 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2015,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nature","title":"Nature","essn":"1476-4687","issn":"0028-0836","nlmid":"0410462","isoabbreviation":"Nature"},"printPublicationDate":"2015-05-01"},"pubYear":"2015","pageInfo":"436-444","abstractText":"Deep learning allows computational models that are composed of multiple processing layers to learn representations of data with multiple levels of abstraction. These methods have dramatically improved the state-of-the-art in speech recognition, visual object recognition, object detection and many other domains such as drug discovery and genomics. Deep learning discovers intricate structure in large data sets by using the backpropagation algorithm to indicate how a machine should change its internal parameters that are used to compute the representation in each layer from the representation in the previous layer. Deep convolutional nets have brought about breakthroughs in processing images, video, speech and audio, whereas recurrent nets have shone light on sequential data such as text and speech.","affiliation":"1] Facebook AI Research, 770 Broadway, New York, New York 10003 USA. [2] New York University, 715 Broadway, New York, New York 10003, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Review","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Language"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Algorithms"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Computers"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neural Networks, Computer"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://hal.science/hal-04206682"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14539"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":19522,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2015-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2015-05-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-10","firstPublicationDate":"2015-05-01","firstIndexDate":"2015-05-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"9500320","source":"MED","pmid":"9500320","doi":"10.1016/s0140-6736(97)11096-0","title":"Ileal-lymphoid-nodular hyperplasia, non-specific colitis, and pervasive developmental disorder in children.","authorString":"Wakefield AJ, Murch SH, Anthony A, Linnell J, Casson DM, Malik M, Berelowitz M, Dhillon AP, Thomson MA, Harvey P, Valentine A, Davies SE, Walker-Smith JA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wakefield AJ","lastName":"Wakefield","firstName":"A J","initials":"AJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease Study Group, University Department of Medicine, Royal Free Hospital and School of Medicine, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Murch SH","lastName":"Murch","firstName":"S H","initials":"SH"},{"fullName":"Anthony A","lastName":"Anthony","firstName":"A","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Linnell J","lastName":"Linnell","firstName":"J","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Casson DM","lastName":"Casson","firstName":"D M","initials":"DM"},{"fullName":"Malik M","lastName":"Malik","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Berelowitz M","lastName":"Berelowitz","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Dhillon AP","lastName":"Dhillon","firstName":"A P","initials":"AP"},{"fullName":"Thomson MA","lastName":"Thomson","firstName":"M A","initials":"MA"},{"fullName":"Harvey P","lastName":"Harvey","firstName":"P","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Valentine A","lastName":"Valentine","firstName":"A","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Davies SE","lastName":"Davies","firstName":"S E","initials":"SE"},{"fullName":"Walker-Smith JA","lastName":"Walker-Smith","firstName":"J A","initials":"JA"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9103","volume":"351","journalIssueId":1194087,"dateOfPublication":"1998 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":1998,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Lancet","title":"Lancet (London, England)","essn":"1474-547X","issn":"0140-6736","nlmid":"2985213R","isoabbreviation":"Lancet"},"printPublicationDate":"1998-02-01"},"pubYear":"1998","pageInfo":"637-641","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>We investigated a consecutive series of children with chronic enterocolitis and regressive developmental disorder.<h4>Methods</h4>12 children (mean age 6 years [range 3-10], 11 boys) were referred to a paediatric gastroenterology unit with a history of normal development followed by loss of acquired skills, including language, together with diarrhoea and abdominal pain. Children underwent gastroenterological, neurological, and developmental assessment and review of developmental records. Ileocolonoscopy and biopsy sampling, magnetic-resonance imaging (MRI), electroencephalography (EEG), and lumbar puncture were done under sedation. Barium follow-through radiography was done where possible. Biochemical, haematological, and immunological profiles were examined.<h4>Findings</h4>Onset of behavioural symptoms was associated, by the parents, with measles, mumps, and rubella vaccination in eight of the 12 children, with measles infection in one child, and otitis media in another. All 12 children had intestinal abnormalities, ranging from lymphoid nodular hyperplasia to aphthoid ulceration. Histology showed patchy chronic inflammation in the colon in 11 children and reactive ileal lymphoid hyperplasia in seven, but no granulomas. Behavioural disorders included autism (nine), disintegrative psychosis (one), and possible postviral or vaccinal encephalitis (two). There were no focal neurological abnormalities and MRI and EEG tests were normal. Abnormal laboratory results were significantly raised urinary methylmalonic acid compared with age-matched controls (p=0.003), low haemoglobin in four children, and a low serum IgA in four children.<h4>Interpretation</h4>We identified associated gastrointestinal disease and developmental regression in a group of previously normal children, which was generally associated in time with possible environmental triggers.","affiliation":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease Study Group, University Department of Medicine, Royal Free Hospital and School of Medicine, London, UK.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Retracted Publication","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ileum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Lymphoid Tissue","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Measles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Enterocolitis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Otitis Media","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hyperplasia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Vaccines, Combined","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Measles-Mumps-Rubella Vaccine"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Measles Vaccine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mumps Vaccine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rubella Vaccine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Developmental Disabilities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Measles Vaccine","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Rubella Vaccine","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Mumps Vaccine","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Vaccines, Combined","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Measles-Mumps-Rubella Vaccine","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0140-6736(97)11096-0"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"9500313","reference":"Lancet. 1998 Feb 28;351(9103):611-2. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(05)78423-3","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1},{"source":"MED","id":"9525390","reference":"Lancet. 1998 Mar 21;351(9106):905; author reply 908-9.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":2},{"source":"MED","id":"9525391","reference":"Lancet. 1998 Mar 21;351(9106):905-6; author reply 908-9. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(05)70316-0","type":"Comment in","orderIn":3},